      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Social Capital Theory has evolved significantly over the years, with contributions from numerous scholars. Pierre Bourdieu's work on social capital has been foundational, emphasizing the role of social networks and cultural capital in social stratification. James Coleman's theories further expanded on the concept by highlighting the importance of social structures in fostering cooperation and trust. Robert Putnam's research, particularly his book 'Bowling Alone,' has been instrumental in examining the decline of social capital in contemporary societies. Nan Lin's contributions have focused on the mechanisms of social capital, including trust and reciprocity. Alejandro Portes has explored the intersection of social capital with immigration and transnationalism. Robert D. Putnam, along with Francis Fukuyama, has delved into the implications of social capital for democracy and civic engagement. Mark Granovetter's work on the strength of weak ties has provided insights into the dynamics of social networks. Ronald Burt's research on structural holes and network theory has offered a different perspective on the role of social capital. Leigh van der Bulck's studies on social capital in the context of globalization and migration have added valuable dimensions to the field. Misako Chiba's research has examined the role of social capital in the labor market, while Stephen J. Gould",
      "source": "target",
